1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,300
Hello everyone, I'm Yui.

2
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,520
My last video blew me away so hard I'm still a bit dizzy.

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:08,927
Today I'll show you how to make your first video with Opus 5.5.

4
00:00:08,927 --> 00:00:11,940
Opus 5.5 was released on September 22,

5
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:14,700
and it's arguably the most capable model right now.

6
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,627
After launch, its price also dropped by 20%,

7
00:00:19,627 --> 00:00:23,500
but nobody expected its vision to be this strong.

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,120
Back when GPT-6 came out,

9
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:31,673
it could do 3D modeling, which is really impressive.

10
00:00:31,673 --> 00:00:35,687
Like in this clip: it modeled a living room and a study,

11
00:00:35,687 --> 00:00:39,873
and you can follow the camera and move around freely, like a game.

12
00:00:39,873 --> 00:00:42,767
But nobody expected Opus 5.5 to be this capable too.

13
00:00:42,767 --> 00:00:47,180
Officially, it's the strongest model for vision and computer use,

14
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:51,200
reading charts, screenshots and diagrams in fine detail.

15
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:56,093
So a lot of people ask: how can Claude make videos?

16
00:00:56,093 --> 00:00:58,413
Claude itself is a multimodal model,

17
00:00:58,413 --> 00:01:01,553
but it can't generate audio, images or video.

18
00:01:01,553 --> 00:01:02,933
So I asked Claude,

19
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:05,740
and it said it draws every frame itself,

20
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,013
every single pixel.

21
00:01:08,013 --> 00:01:11,060
Like in this picture, pixel by pixel,

22
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:15,113
using colors it defined beforehand, like 01 or EF.

23
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:16,573
Honestly, it's wild.

24
00:01:16,573 --> 00:01:20,260
After drawing, it screenshots its own work and checks it with its own vision,

25
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:22,647
to see if it matches what I asked for.

26
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:24,220
Here are a few examples.

27
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:29,147
The first example: I used this prompt to have it make a Gintama anime,

28
00:01:29,147 --> 00:01:33,470
picking classic scenes from the whole main storyline.

29
00:01:34,687 --> 00:01:37,277
The second is Naruto. It did a great job too.

30
00:01:38,907 --> 00:01:39,800
The third is JoJo.

31
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:44,063
Similar prompt, also asking for some basic scenes.

32
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:49,340
The fourth is my earlier video ranking AI models, from god-tier to trash.

33
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:52,020
Look at what's in this video:

34
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:56,400
the voice, the colors, even the character's mouth

35
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,920
opening and closing with the audio.

36
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,273
It checked all of that itself, by taking screenshots and looking.

37
00:02:02,273 --> 00:02:03,507
Pretty shocking.

38
00:02:03,507 --> 00:02:06,587
So if Claude isn't a diffusion model, where do these videos come from?

39
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:09,767
Since Claude isn't a diffusion model and draws frame by frame,

40
00:02:09,767 --> 00:02:11,993
how does a diffusion model work?

41
00:02:11,993 --> 00:02:14,640
Diffusion models aren't good at generating text,

42
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:18,800
because they restore an image out of random noise,

43
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:20,707
or a video.

44
00:02:20,707 --> 00:02:23,480
But a large language model writes one word at a time.

45
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,520
So, in my personal opinion,

46
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:29,607
diffusion models don't suit images with text, or lots of text.

47
00:02:29,607 --> 00:02:31,980
Those really should go to LLMs,

48
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,670
like Opus 5.5, generating it piece by piece.

49
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:36,247
Which is really counterintuitive:

50
00:02:36,247 --> 00:02:38,433
an LLM making videos.

51
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:41,533
As I said, it makes 30 images per second,

52
00:02:41,533 --> 00:02:42,847
plus an audio track.

53
00:02:42,847 --> 00:02:44,980
So 20 seconds means 600 drawn images.

54
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:47,813
It puts them in an HTML page,

55
00:02:47,813 --> 00:02:50,340
and once every frame is drawn, it renders them

56
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:52,040
with ffmpeg.

57
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:55,933
A video is really just lots of images stitched together.

58
00:02:55,933 --> 00:02:58,487
Claude starts by drawing each image,

59
00:02:58,487 --> 00:03:00,687
puts them in a browser once they're drawn,

60
00:03:00,687 --> 00:03:04,240
turns the images into a video with ffmpeg,

61
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:07,080
and then samples frames to check it.

62
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,240
So why do I think a coding model is better for making videos?

63
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,200
Because a coding LLM

64
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:17,520
knows what text to write, where it goes, when it appears and how it moves.

65
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:21,300
A language model can pin all of that down precisely.

66
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:24,973
Diffusion models are better for things like 3D rendering,

67
00:03:24,973 --> 00:03:27,573
such as game cutscenes,

68
00:03:27,573 --> 00:03:29,327
or AI short dramas.

69
00:03:29,327 --> 00:03:31,807
This next example is a video I made,

70
00:03:31,807 --> 00:03:33,920
so let me show you how it was made.

71
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:36,900
It has a character in the bottom-left,

72
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:40,987
handwritten text appearing, a table of contents, and a progress bar.

73
00:03:40,987 --> 00:03:44,000
First it writes the script, and once it's done,

74
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,387
I review it.

75
00:03:45,387 --> 00:03:48,407
The script covers when scenes change, what's on screen,

76
00:03:48,407 --> 00:03:51,667
what the subtitles say, and what tone the voiceover takes.

77
00:03:51,667 --> 00:03:53,693
I set all of that up for it.

78
00:03:53,693 --> 00:03:58,187
Then I send it the audio, either AI voice or my own recording.

79
00:03:58,187 --> 00:04:03,053
It then builds the video around the audio's intensity,

80
00:04:03,053 --> 00:04:06,387
matching the audio to the video frame by frame.

81
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:09,847
Take the character in the bottom-left: it has lots of moves,

82
00:04:09,847 --> 00:04:12,927
like counting one-two-three, hands on hips, and cheering 'yay'.

83
00:04:12,927 --> 00:04:14,687
I use about 30 of those per episode,

84
00:04:14,687 --> 00:04:19,340
plus small gestures, nods at sentence ends and glances at the content, added automatically.

85
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:22,267
All of this is made by the AI itself.

86
00:04:22,267 --> 00:04:25,833
The BGM and sound effects are synthesized by Claude in Python,

87
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:27,180
which is really convenient.

88
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:29,540
But that's just the baseline.

89
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:34,133
If you want higher quality,

90
00:04:34,133 --> 00:04:36,440
you can keep looking for better voices and music.

91
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:39,147
So after I'm done, Claude also samples frames to look,

92
00:04:39,147 --> 00:04:40,400
and listens.

93
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,547
Now let's compare the cost of making videos with a video model

94
00:04:43,547 --> 00:04:46,860
versus with an LLM.

95
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:50,260
Right now the best is clearly Seedance 2.5 from China,

96
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:51,267
but it isn't cheap.

97
00:04:51,267 --> 00:04:54,540
Google's Veo 3.1 isn't as capable,

98
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:55,700
and it's still expensive.

99
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:57,787
For the same 6-minute video,

100
00:04:57,787 --> 00:05:00,433
a language model comes with coding plans,

101
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:02,693
so it's far cheaper.

102
00:05:02,693 --> 00:05:05,033
A six-minute video usually uses

103
00:05:05,033 --> 00:05:09,667
about 5–10% of my weekly 20x plan quota.

104
00:05:09,667 --> 00:05:11,867
And these assets are reusable:

105
00:05:11,867 --> 00:05:13,800
once the character assets are made,

106
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:15,567
the next video doesn't need to make them again.

107
00:05:15,567 --> 00:05:18,133
Everything can be distilled into a skill,

108
00:05:18,133 --> 00:05:20,133
so you can churn them out in batches.

109
00:05:20,133 --> 00:05:23,713
For example, the news digest I wanted to make:

110
00:05:23,713 --> 00:05:27,093
a daily tech-news roundup video

111
00:05:27,093 --> 00:05:29,753
that runs automatically every day.

112
00:05:29,753 --> 00:05:32,153
About reusing character assets:

113
00:05:32,153 --> 00:05:35,160
if the models keep improving,

114
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:40,613
drawing anime with smooth, continuous lines more precisely,

115
00:05:40,613 --> 00:05:43,480
or even realistic human styles,

116
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:44,960
then all these assets are reusable,

117
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:47,753
and reusable assets have real value.

118
00:05:47,753 --> 00:05:48,913
It's not a gacha pull.

119
00:05:48,913 --> 00:05:53,033
If you're a programmer who wants to try making videos,

120
00:05:53,033 --> 00:05:54,933
I think it costs almost nothing.

121
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:57,713
The models in Codex or Claude Code

122
00:05:57,713 --> 00:05:59,053
are capable enough, I think.

123
00:05:59,053 --> 00:06:02,673
Some Chinese models can do this too,

124
00:06:02,673 --> 00:06:04,427
but the results aren't great.

125
00:06:04,427 --> 00:06:07,860
Rendering just runs locally on the CPU.

126
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:09,533
OK, let's wrap up.

127
00:06:09,533 --> 00:06:11,080
Thanks for watching.

128
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:13,300
If you're interested, give it a try.

129
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,947
I'll put the two prompts from the beginning in the comments,

130
00:06:15,947 --> 00:06:17,627
so check them out there.

131
00:06:17,627 --> 00:06:18,417
Bye!

